      "ballot_id": "B004",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michel Della Negra", "Peter Jenni", "Tejinder S. Virdee"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The experimental discovery of the Higgs boson confirmed a central prediction of particle physics and stands among the field's defining achievements.",
        "attribution": "These leaders were instrumental in building and directing the ATLAS and CMS efforts that made the discovery possible. Credit for a result of this scale is necessarily collective.",
        "maturity": "The discovery has been independently confirmed and extensively studied over more than a decade.",
        "uncertainties": ["How to represent the essential contributions of the large international collaborations within the prize's individual-recipient limit."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks transformed precision measurement, enabling unprecedented timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics.",
        "attribution": "Katori's lattice-clock proposal and realization and Ye's independent experimental advances were both central to establishing the method.",
        "maturity": "The technique has been repeatedly realized and refined across laboratories, with clear scientific and practical value.",
        "uncertainties": ["The boundary between the original clock concept and later advances that brought its performance to maturity."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Federico Capasso", "Jérôme Faist"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser was a major semiconductor-laser invention, opening tunable mid- and far-infrared sources with broad scientific and technological use.",
        "attribution": "Faist and Capasso were central to the invention and first demonstration. The named attribution should be checked against the contributions of other members of the original team.",
        "maturity": "The device principle has been validated through decades of development and widespread use.",
        "uncertainties": ["The longlist's credited names may not capture every contributor to the original invention and demonstration."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Akira Ohtomo", "Harold Y. Hwang", "Jochen Mannhart"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of a conducting two-dimensional electron gas at an oxide interface revealed an unexpected electronic state and helped establish oxide heterostructures as a rich platform for new physics.",
        "attribution": "Ohtomo and Hwang are closely tied to the original interface result; Mannhart's contribution to the experimental field is substantial, though the precise division of credit needs careful historical review.",
        "maturity": "The phenomenon has been studied in many oxide systems, although its microscopic origin and the extent of its generality remain active questions.",
        "uncertainties": ["The extent to which the original observations and later explanations support the same claim of discovery."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B023",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Kane", "Eugene J. Mele", "Laurens W. Molenkamp"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The topological-insulator framework and quantum spin Hall effect revealed new phases of matter with protected boundary states and substantial experimental and conceptual impact.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele provided foundational theory, while Molenkamp's experiments demonstrated the quantum spin Hall state. The link between these distinct contributions should be made explicit.",
        "maturity": "The early results have been followed by extensive experimental confirmation and a broad body of work on topological materials.",
        "uncertainties": ["Whether the award should recognize the prediction and first demonstration together or a narrower discovery."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Eli Yablonovitch", "John D. Joannopoulos", "Sajeev John"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals established the control of light propagation through periodic structures, a powerful idea with lasting influence in optics and materials design.",
        "attribution": "Yablonovitch and John independently articulated key routes to photonic band gaps; Joannopoulos helped develop the theoretical and computational understanding.",
        "maturity": "The underlying physics is well established, with many experimental realizations and applications.",
        "uncertainties": ["The parallel origin of the central proposals makes the scope and division of credit important."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B027",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Alexandre Blais", "Andreas Wallraff", "Robert J. Schoelkopf"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit quantum electrodynamics brought cavity quantum optics into engineered superconducting circuits and became a foundation for controlling light and matter at the quantum level.",
        "attribution": "Blais established the theoretical framework, while Wallraff and Schoelkopf were central to influential experimental demonstrations and development.",
        "maturity": "The field has produced robust, replicated control techniques and is central to current quantum-device research.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field's progress is broad and cumulative, so the most prize-worthy original contribution needs precise definition."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Artur Ekert", "Charles H. Bennett", "Gilles Brassard"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum key distribution connected foundational quantum principles to secure communication and motivated major advances in quantum information science.",
        "attribution": "Bennett and Brassard introduced a landmark prepare-and-measure protocol; Ekert introduced a distinct entanglement-based approach. Their contributions are significant but not identical.",
        "maturity": "The core protocols have been repeatedly demonstrated, though real-world implementations require attention to devices and security assumptions.",
        "uncertainties": ["Whether these distinct protocols form a sufficiently unified prize contribution and how much weight to place on later implementation."]
      }
    }
  ]
